# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT NEVER GOES TO GITHUB.
#
# ---- READ THIS BEFORE YOU LOOSEN ANYTHING BELOW ----
#
# THIS REPOSITORY IS PUBLIC. Gigi's call, Aug 24 2026.
#
# And git is not like the Netlify near-miss at v3.72, where a file could be
# taken back out of `public/` and the problem was over. GIT KEEPS HISTORY.
# Committing a file once and deleting it in the next commit does NOT remove it:
# it stays in the repository for ever, in every clone anyone has ever made, and
# a public repo is scraped by machines within hours of being pushed.
#
# So the rule for this file is stricter than for `public/`:
#
#   IF IT NAMES AZIANNA OR MEASURES HER, IT DOES NOT GO IN.
#
# `check-publish-safety` asserts the rules below rather than trusting them.
# Deleting a line here will fail the build, on purpose. If you mean to change
# one, change the check too, and write down why and when — the same way every
# other reversal in this project is recorded.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ---- HER DATA. THE WHOLE REASON THIS FILE EXISTS. -------------------------
#
# Each of these parses as a full export: her learner name, all nine measured
# strand levels, 74 answers, and both journal entries in her own words.
#
# There were THIRTEEN copies of it in this folder on Aug 24 2026.

local/
*her-latest-export*.json
*her-backup*.json
her-*.json

# ---- ⚠️ ADDED Aug 26 2026, AND IT WAS ALREADY LIVE WHEN IT WAS ADDED ----
#
# Gigi made a "Backup folder" to keep her exports in, which is exactly the right
# habit — and the file she put in it is called
# `petal-pestle-backup-2026-08-26.json`.
#
# NONE OF THE FOUR PATTERNS ABOVE MATCH THAT NAME. `*her-backup*.json` needs the
# words "her-backup"; hers says "petal-pestle-backup". So a 93KB file carrying 99
# rows of her record — her name, her nine measured levels, her journal in her own
# words — was sitting in the folder, unignored, one commit away from a PUBLIC
# repository. check-publish-safety caught it, which is the entire reason it walks
# every JSON on disk and identifies an export by its STRUCTURE rather than by its
# name (v3.73's lesson: a word scan misses a renamed file).
#
# So the FOLDER is excluded, not a filename pattern. She will name the next one
# whatever the app suggests, and a rule that depends on her choosing a filename
# is a rule that fails the first time she does not.
Backup folder/
Backup Folder/
backup folder/

# The scratch copies from v3.72's negative tests — eleven of them. This folder
# is meant to be moved out of the project entirely; the ignore is the belt as
# well as the braces.
_to_delete/
_to_delete-from-app/
_archive-test/


# ---- HER RECORDS — THE MISSION CONTROL RULE ------------------------------
#
# ⚠️ `claude/` IS COMMITTED, MINUS THREE FILES. Gigi, Aug 24 2026, after
# showing that Mission Control is already set up this way: mirror Lamar
# exactly. His repository commits `docs/` — PROJECT_LOG, MASTER_PLAN_REVIEW,
# THE_SCHEDULE — and excludes the real student records even so.
#
# HIS .gitignore STATES THE RULE, AND IT IS THE RIGHT ONE:
#
#   "The repository is PRIVATE, but 'private' is a setting someone can change
#    by accident and a collaborator can be added to. ANYTHING THAT WOULD BE A
#    PROBLEM IN PUBLIC DOES NOT GO IN AT ALL."
#
# ---- ⚠️ CORRECTED Aug 26 2026: THIS REPOSITORY IS PUBLIC. ----
#
# The paragraph above is quoted from Lamar's .gitignore and HIS repository is
# private. This one is not, and Gigi confirmed on Aug 26 that it stays public.
#
# So privacy is not the second guard here. IT IS NOT A GUARD AT ALL. There is
# exactly one guard, it is this file, and check-publish-safety is what asserts
# it. Every sentence below that used to lean on "and anyway it is private" has
# been rewritten, because a reason that is not true is worse than no reason —
# it is the sentence that talks the next person out of being careful.
#
# Mission Control's rule matters MORE here, not less: anything that would be a
# problem in public does not go in at all. Here it IS in public.
#
# The first and only guard is that the three files below are never committed:
#
#   · her export and her backup — her raw record, both already caught above
#   · azianna-diagnostic-results.md — a FULL EDUCATIONAL ASSESSMENT of a named
#     nine-year-old: all nine measured strand levels, the item-level detail
#     behind each one, and how often she needed a question read aloud. It is
#     the most sensitive document in this project by a distance, and nothing in
#     the app reads it. It stays on Gigi's machine, where the next session
#     works from it.
#
# The master plan, the build log and the blueprints DO go in. They are the best
# writing in this project and losing them was a real cost.
#
# ⚠️ THEY QUOTE HER FIRST NAME AND HER LEVELS, AND THEY ARE PUBLIC. Gigi was
# shown the count on Aug 26 and decided knowingly. What she was told, so that
# the next person is deciding from the same facts rather than re-opening it:
#
#   · Her FIRST NAME only. Her record stores learnerName "PrettyGlow", so the
#     app itself never renders a full name. No surname, no address, no photo,
#     no date of birth, no school.
#   · It is in about 60 committed files, not two — the master plan (18), the
#     build log (16), src/config/buildStamp.js (17) and ~55 others with one or
#     two each. Removing the two documents would not have solved it.
#   · ⚠️ src/config/buildStamp.js RENDERS ON THE LIVE SITE. ParentDashboard
#     prints the whole changelog in the Grown-Up Corner, and the Grown-Up
#     Corner is a passcode a visitor sets themselves — ParentGate says so in
#     its own header: "a lock on a door, not a safe". netlify.toml records
#     that the site cannot be password-protected on the free plan. So her name
#     and her levels are readable by anyone with the URL, and that was true
#     before the repository question was ever asked.
#   · The people who actually open it are her mother and her grandmother. The
#     URL is unlisted and nothing links to it.
#
# ⚠️ NONE OF THAT MAKES THE FILES BELOW SAFE TO COMMIT. A first name beside a
# grade level is a different thing from a full educational assessment with the
# item detail behind every number. The line is drawn below, and it holds
# whatever the repository visibility is.

claude/azianna-diagnostic-results.md


# ---- BUILD OUTPUT --------------------------------------------------------
#
# Netlify runs `npm run build` itself, so `dist/` is made fresh on their
# machine from the committed source. Committing it means the site and the code
# can silently disagree about which version is live — and `dist/` has carried
# her export before (v3.72).

dist/
node_modules/
.vite/
*.local


